[{:db/id "q-48523/selection/7b1b8538b264eef6681d0ab5f3b14d51b6cdff67f34fba59d0886ae5c6acf80a" :answer/id #uuid "049543f9-59b7-5d2e-8a72-1f76f7dc7ba6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48523/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "a19bcd32-5a0b-5762-ab36-42f8e4e845b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48523/selection/7d5cb2f332b3f301a23fe106d8ba723a7bd83bf838e8df1d59ab973ad8f00e0d" :answer/id #uuid "f599c537-d66f-5f0b-8c24-f059432a592f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48523/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" :answer/id #uuid "70ca07c0-3208-5e8a-a5e1-53d8e80e29f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48523/selection/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d" :answer/id #uuid "09a5f3e4-6e4e-57e3-933e-ecc823375b8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48523/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a4e5326-e5aa-5228-9be2-f8130adba292" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48523/selection/7d5cb2f332b3f301a23fe106d8ba723a7bd83bf838e8df1d59ab973ad8f00e0d" :answer-field/choices ["q-48523/selection/7b1b8538b264eef6681d0ab5f3b14d51b6cdff67f34fba59d0886ae5c6acf80a" "q-48523/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-48523/selection/7d5cb2f332b3f301a23fe106d8ba723a7bd83bf838e8df1d59ab973ad8f00e0d" "q-48523/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" "q-48523/selection/2edf353dc47d221855e5e7ee4be942392390ff01766e089c29dc368c2051b12d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48523" :question/id #uuid "df002dfb-f43a-5d15-a121-3e41ea165d9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-48523" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to one third of the rate of increase of the diameter. Determine the radius of the circle at that moment." :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{D}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-48523/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "72b11864-0846-5b22-aa45-5a20079afafb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48523"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-115919/selection/a78357ed475b02ea14405524d42913261868b11f90601f9d921e9581a6fb810a" :answer/id #uuid "b682e1dd-880a-5171-8fef-810a6e2f91f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115919/selection/bba1281a570f0a5f7c6a3332e57a3cdd84cc93ef6782c0069189c1c1d144d8a6" :answer/id #uuid "e48dbf8b-7038-5b39-b444-4332d86a0587" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115919/selection/868e106f2c32519524df25ed990b31b89fc1a334fedad7daaa8ba8be32dd890f" :answer/id #uuid "789f57b5-c846-5ce6-ba04-e38ac6562e64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115919/selection/7d5cb2f332b3f301a23fe106d8ba723a7bd83bf838e8df1d59ab973ad8f00e0d" :answer/id #uuid "13faf97a-ac74-5300-ab04-adfff1bcf9d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115919/selection/0fc9e01a0ed927de823a69ffd840123490099d2f56e7ba9a6f7c9c1c2d5473e9" :answer/id #uuid "a364baef-5f61-57e1-beb5-a0fdca64582f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-115919/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd1e494-0d7d-5d5c-8bd1-bb9576ef2587" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-115919/selection/868e106f2c32519524df25ed990b31b89fc1a334fedad7daaa8ba8be32dd890f" :answer-field/choices ["q-115919/selection/a78357ed475b02ea14405524d42913261868b11f90601f9d921e9581a6fb810a" "q-115919/selection/bba1281a570f0a5f7c6a3332e57a3cdd84cc93ef6782c0069189c1c1d144d8a6" "q-115919/selection/868e106f2c32519524df25ed990b31b89fc1a334fedad7daaa8ba8be32dd890f" "q-115919/selection/7d5cb2f332b3f301a23fe106d8ba723a7bd83bf838e8df1d59ab973ad8f00e0d" "q-115919/selection/0fc9e01a0ed927de823a69ffd840123490099d2f56e7ba9a6f7c9c1c2d5473e9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-115919" :question/id #uuid "e4a753a6-f7b9-5a6d-be3f-8697734caaa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-115919" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to three-fifths of the rate of increase of the diameter. Determine the radius of the circle at that moment." :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{D}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{6}{5\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{5\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-115919/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "72b11864-0846-5b22-aa45-5a20079afafb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-115919"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344661/field-1/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer/id #uuid "3b154c70-f690-5597-a49a-fa5087b1173c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344661/field-1/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "a770eb62-bc0e-5c01-be8e-fc49015c2a61" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344661/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ed5e393-37f8-5a8f-a7d8-3a647f3b2806" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344661/field-1/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" :answer-field/choices ["q-344661/field-1/e326a281f601aec64d5dcce4921033c5f93cbf7f8b0a7a5809a9962dfadde666" "q-344661/field-1/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344661" :question/id #uuid "bc1e09a0-52d9-593f-80f3-e2133be81a8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-344661" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to nine times the rate of increase of the circumference. What is the diameter $D$ of the circle at that moment?\n\n$D=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $D$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ 9 & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =18\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =18\\pi  \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =2\\cdot 9 \\\\ D & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344661/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "72b11864-0846-5b22-aa45-5a20079afafb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344661"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-48520/selection/a996cecead059c2e05b8ded2ceb60e4e2170a4344e0c20625524ed13b23c6fbe" :answer/id #uuid "02ab01ef-ea88-5695-9d52-2caed06354d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48520/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" :answer/id #uuid "24ef66b9-6248-5c37-9bf5-944c0d0ab87c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48520/selection/a2bf9f278822239fe980121e1c2efe6b2a1075aef929e587d78abba90e1e8b5d" :answer/id #uuid "51bc57b8-40d3-5768-9b51-f7d20ffc7303" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2\\pi }" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48520/selection/c71471265c459db429c0904073d51b98263c5556bf8642fd8ee00873955333bc" :answer/id #uuid "f2c9838b-0da0-55a7-a4c6-67df46f6062c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48520/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "4897ada0-2c94-5cf0-ba08-41193ffc201e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-48520/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff78f64-2449-5f95-a647-e95decd31f6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-48520/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer-field/choices ["q-48520/selection/a996cecead059c2e05b8ded2ceb60e4e2170a4344e0c20625524ed13b23c6fbe" "q-48520/selection/f00d8b7a5eeed0dcaa02b7f16c350647a90a461726a760f9ecdf0379dbf8fa63" "q-48520/selection/a2bf9f278822239fe980121e1c2efe6b2a1075aef929e587d78abba90e1e8b5d" "q-48520/selection/c71471265c459db429c0904073d51b98263c5556bf8642fd8ee00873955333bc" "q-48520/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-48520" :question/id #uuid "b8d48815-34e5-5703-a8f9-0f1af6101d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-48520" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to two-thirds of the rate of increase of the circumference. What is the radius of the circle at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-48520/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "72b11864-0846-5b22-aa45-5a20079afafb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-48520"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344670/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "57f6e8ec-9891-5480-82c2-33d4f0e90a10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344670/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4aeb03c8-5ee3-5018-9e58-d5fe767aa350" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344670/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-344670/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344670" :question/id #uuid "66bf1096-af7c-5580-859b-acc430a4a2c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-344670" :question/problem "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to three times the rate of increase of the circumference. What is the radius $r$ of the circle at that moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =6\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C=2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =6\\pi  \\\\ r & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-344670/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "72b11864-0846-5b22-aa45-5a20079afafb"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344670"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186064574 :question/answer-fields 17592186061955] {:db/id "q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283/8ef952f659d840ee973be6159d4bf725e84f4141311033002fbbb71fb05c5453" :answer/id #uuid "db71d2a7-6874-5160-a105-e1a328fbc551" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283/6c1eadd6571e2cf7dd521a568acb4575abcc21a597480f9b78ca545bd5a6766b" :answer/id #uuid "4bf1d7e2-a927-50a1-bc41-b3c069333071" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\text{ cm}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283/2c9c0abfe50803bfd0144c2588c8a31c38b05908bedc4a38ce9a9f7ca1e8288e" :answer/id #uuid "cd23a5c2-9b3e-524a-9790-eea5e14d2f3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283/fe18e02e64f5f656a1bcde0b52e73af4556f1674d98cd0da7d4f374604c8143b" :answer/id #uuid "724af7b3-4316-5636-b16e-4e109a722dfb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283/3f814a1c310d5a86de7f23881361b6e8e1ffbc0d2a194890dd687915bd6c5c95" :answer/id #uuid "cbd607fc-fce0-5a30-bfd4-cd2abc231184" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\text{ cm}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283" :answer-field/id #uuid "fcd5a7bc-706b-5423-b1ff-65f5cf1a8d98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283/2c9c0abfe50803bfd0144c2588c8a31c38b05908bedc4a38ce9a9f7ca1e8288e" :answer-field/choices ["q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283/8ef952f659d840ee973be6159d4bf725e84f4141311033002fbbb71fb05c5453" "q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283/6c1eadd6571e2cf7dd521a568acb4575abcc21a597480f9b78ca545bd5a6766b" "q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283/2c9c0abfe50803bfd0144c2588c8a31c38b05908bedc4a38ce9a9f7ca1e8288e" "q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283/fe18e02e64f5f656a1bcde0b52e73af4556f1674d98cd0da7d4f374604c8143b" "q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283/3f814a1c310d5a86de7f23881361b6e8e1ffbc0d2a194890dd687915bd6c5c95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-2547"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/d4c0faafff52f414c30a4da32f9f0ba3520ef88d7c8bebe5c12a7ccea3109d17.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a floor forms a circular puddle. The area of the puddle increases at a rate of $40\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ How fast is the radius of the puddle increasing at the moment when the radius is $5\\text{ cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=5,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=40\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=40\\pi $ and $r=5,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi (5) \\\\ 40\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 10\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius is increasing at a rate of $4$ centimeters per minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-2547/selection/ma-v1/777ec6b70fb9e7f0aa3597a4d96a3ffe5df7ecd2cd34c94e92589e9f591e1283"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-344724/field-1/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer/id #uuid "664fa3b9-b222-5122-91ad-68f24550d527" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344724/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10" :answer/id #uuid "8561063b-eb23-54af-abc9-22676b0b0b77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344724/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2bb8d963-9f74-5e95-965d-b85d83733110" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344724/field-1/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" :answer-field/choices ["q-344724/field-1/b93157e0eb36ec8e645eeabaa0819400f448209e0db24c1abf59704822466393" "q-344724/field-1/c087f5dc559272d193745a4428553203b94404f681d8529e6e619348c0528d10"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344724" :question/id #uuid "94353f0d-8c1e-5849-a6b7-63fe2a4729db" :question/math-academy-id "q-344724" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/7cfd87cefcfc8d89330ba08f41c1a90392a2cea68550c1bb648362a8f65e68b6.png)\n\nA circular ripple spreads on the surface of a pond in a circular shape. The area of the ripple is increasing at a rate of $6\\pi \\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}\\,/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the radius of the ripple increasing at the moment when the radius is $4\\,\\text{ft}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe radius of the ripple is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=4,$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=6\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=6\\pi $ and $r=4,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi (4) \\\\ 6\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344724/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2dbfe46-55f0-5d6a-b410-164f0aa2345f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344724"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-315824/field-1/1be980d47b7c8688003a0125c366085994673fb630263e0102cd536ae08b007a" :answer/id #uuid "e1e9e08e-f8c4-56ab-b399-fa19ad8cb6aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-315824/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5c41a92c-8cfe-52cd-865e-f2f4128913e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-315824/field-1/1be980d47b7c8688003a0125c366085994673fb630263e0102cd536ae08b007a" :answer-field/choices ["q-315824/field-1/1be980d47b7c8688003a0125c366085994673fb630263e0102cd536ae08b007a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-315824" :question/id #uuid "5f12f32c-9ad0-541c-9d58-9375624fa3eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-315824" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/8b8ca01e64d2830bd5fce6a5c0b47b74f955ae1e4a7e37dcd0c7bcaa41ed1383.png)\n\nA small, circular ink blot spreads on a paper. If the blot’s circumference is increasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\,\\text{mm}/\\text{s}$, calculate the rate of change of its area at the moment when the radius is $10\\,\\text{mm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe area of the ink blot is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=10,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=4\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=10\\,\\text{mm},$ $C=2\\pi (10)=20\\pi \\text{ mm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}=4\\pi $ and $C=20\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{20\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =40\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area is increasing at a rate of $40\\pi {\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-315824/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2dbfe46-55f0-5d6a-b410-164f0aa2345f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-315824"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-116650/selection/e99031be2d97ae4e6fe7a123ba3a0d3ade3bb7e7cd79d6c06d11aa8b8f20136a" :answer/id #uuid "c16b8e38-cacc-5f3a-bcc9-faeeb19cb5c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116650/selection/f0cc0a3cf935be46a8b6a1b63837766f0c9826f9374ecc30d76acdd723930611" :answer/id #uuid "effe4b43-f60d-55f6-9827-193efcb5ff2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116650/selection/2c2243c61574ad5ab1651f1cdf3d4596bcc4b1a17ca37757b037ebc245f90ee8" :answer/id #uuid "f17db42a-2796-5833-8562-6c7237f6e30f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116650/selection/831ddb6c4dcd2e1ba875a906f86a6f9498e03750278a0fac5d5c158ff7f43d04" :answer/id #uuid "ac83d279-fbe7-5b5e-b6db-0fc51c57bcb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116650/selection/000126f82fddb7cbb2dbd67c137346ca10a2e3830a2ad29a393748ff301c1930" :answer/id #uuid "c2c7cb7b-592b-548b-ae02-44ad8cb573e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pi \\,\\text{km/year}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-116650/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb6b485b-4493-5e86-8177-185390717567" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-116650/selection/e99031be2d97ae4e6fe7a123ba3a0d3ade3bb7e7cd79d6c06d11aa8b8f20136a" :answer-field/choices ["q-116650/selection/e99031be2d97ae4e6fe7a123ba3a0d3ade3bb7e7cd79d6c06d11aa8b8f20136a" "q-116650/selection/f0cc0a3cf935be46a8b6a1b63837766f0c9826f9374ecc30d76acdd723930611" "q-116650/selection/2c2243c61574ad5ab1651f1cdf3d4596bcc4b1a17ca37757b037ebc245f90ee8" "q-116650/selection/831ddb6c4dcd2e1ba875a906f86a6f9498e03750278a0fac5d5c158ff7f43d04" "q-116650/selection/000126f82fddb7cbb2dbd67c137346ca10a2e3830a2ad29a393748ff301c1930"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-116650" :question/id #uuid "5772907f-89d6-59e3-8211-18050e552f56" :question/math-academy-id "q-116650" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/4fa141d5af6f23e2eaa1d416def8f84e21e48f775b5b195cbc8ed74e4bb64218.png)\n\nA circular iceberg is melting in such a way that the superficial circle is decreasing its area at a rate of $24\\pi \\,{\\text{km}}^{2}\\text{/year}.$ Calculate the rate at which the circumference of the iceberg is decreasing at the moment when the radius is  $6\\,\\text{km}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=6,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=6\\,\\text{km},$ $C=2\\pi (6)=12\\pi \\text{ km}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-24\\pi $ and $C=12\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{12\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{24\\pi }{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\text{ km/year}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-116650/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2dbfe46-55f0-5d6a-b410-164f0aa2345f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-116650"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-22997/selection/46d86f9a6af6537b3cf4fd6da3b0e7cd9180d6975f2565324460edfc8ef213a9" :answer/id #uuid "35715347-43b0-5949-9bf9-759c7a5fb563" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pi \\,\\text{mm}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22997/selection/50b65034a980aeecbdd9f4a99b65b27447f6104f7e2b7462cbb5d8450cdfeb9f" :answer/id #uuid "cac2ed53-277f-5d8b-aecb-8793e9e285c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{mm}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22997/selection/c899b2796931b460d533b5d8abe3e40a31137d331252832d34c2000c064bacc8" :answer/id #uuid "5a316171-5ee4-564a-aef8-500a5e2bf5df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22997/selection/2120043adc07819a3ce1fca28a2ca0f6f129186cd2b71de62713f4ea381a9710" :answer/id #uuid "617488a0-6ab7-5849-bb4e-12441cdf4c3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22997/selection/bcf18e642a79dd1a82a1b59f4abebe085228ff1b14ab9a1571c92c8d558254c2" :answer/id #uuid "4eac0ac3-3e34-5f58-a992-ee198994e4d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi \\,\\text{mm}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-22997/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d3c34ed-f031-579a-a481-4bf6b5eac511" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-22997/selection/46d86f9a6af6537b3cf4fd6da3b0e7cd9180d6975f2565324460edfc8ef213a9" :answer-field/choices ["q-22997/selection/46d86f9a6af6537b3cf4fd6da3b0e7cd9180d6975f2565324460edfc8ef213a9" "q-22997/selection/50b65034a980aeecbdd9f4a99b65b27447f6104f7e2b7462cbb5d8450cdfeb9f" "q-22997/selection/c899b2796931b460d533b5d8abe3e40a31137d331252832d34c2000c064bacc8" "q-22997/selection/2120043adc07819a3ce1fca28a2ca0f6f129186cd2b71de62713f4ea381a9710" "q-22997/selection/bcf18e642a79dd1a82a1b59f4abebe085228ff1b14ab9a1571c92c8d558254c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-22997" :question/id #uuid "55951424-fc68-5e7f-aec1-0a790e663e95" :question/math-academy-id "q-22997" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/701c883ef9f721f9dad8fb59236fbbd297cfac3d3d8845a18d3493d788f8d8a8.png)\n\nPaint is leaking from a tank and is forming a circle on the floor. If the area of the circle is increasing at the rate of $12\\pi \\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{min}$, how fast is the circumference of the circle increasing at the moment when the radius is $4\\,\\text{mm}?$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=4,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=12\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=4\\,\\text{mm},$ $C=2\\pi (4)=8\\pi \\text{ mm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=12\\pi $ and $C=8\\pi $, and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{8\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ 12\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12\\pi }{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is increasing at a rate of $3\\pi \\text{ mm/min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-22997/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2dbfe46-55f0-5d6a-b410-164f0aa2345f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-22997"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-2876/selection/c7c3ed0d96ab30ef0fbd405026ec91862e5d369b2bb71cbe1560d3570ef0aa9c" :answer/id #uuid "ad759edc-1430-544a-ba6d-bb1a400fda54" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2876/selection/3993fbe272fd5e612f7fa6f26890d74d5908adb469e58e6b0d552ab1378f2505" :answer/id #uuid "4547a9c7-562d-59e9-be7c-825e1c9afe20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2876/selection/aa86467425d2c6d0b9351432f6deb600630da992080a56ceffb337cdb070a470" :answer/id #uuid "9556ca5e-9f27-59b9-8ee7-e1f1d72e6934" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2876/selection/0bcb368da7b2b1d9f47bfe5eec92cfd2144ff62d78511121713d24ef26b320b8" :answer/id #uuid "641ad0ed-25fe-5959-a362-4ac0a0be0360" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2876/selection/f9cd564ab279d26252caa1471967e69992139f7dc6e7e33f1ee5b94684dffaa1" :answer/id #uuid "7cdb9734-dc10-5e3e-b8ef-83e102a011b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-2876/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a853cb73-61f7-5e71-b215-ab3c86ee709a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-2876/selection/c7c3ed0d96ab30ef0fbd405026ec91862e5d369b2bb71cbe1560d3570ef0aa9c" :answer-field/choices ["q-2876/selection/c7c3ed0d96ab30ef0fbd405026ec91862e5d369b2bb71cbe1560d3570ef0aa9c" "q-2876/selection/3993fbe272fd5e612f7fa6f26890d74d5908adb469e58e6b0d552ab1378f2505" "q-2876/selection/aa86467425d2c6d0b9351432f6deb600630da992080a56ceffb337cdb070a470" "q-2876/selection/0bcb368da7b2b1d9f47bfe5eec92cfd2144ff62d78511121713d24ef26b320b8" "q-2876/selection/f9cd564ab279d26252caa1471967e69992139f7dc6e7e33f1ee5b94684dffaa1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-2876" :question/id #uuid "8902e1cf-8ac8-56b7-9562-e968fa619d35" :question/math-academy-id "q-2876" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/2bd252d1bbecdb6cf3d15af44d5599f9694dbbfc339241f90ed2e49cfb279000.png)\n\nA giant inflatable ball is losing air uniformly, causing its radius to change at a rate of $\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}.$ If the radius at a certain moment is $24\\,\\text{in},$ what is the rate at which the surface area is decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $A$ its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=24\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}8\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }$ and $r=24.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{12\\pi }\\cdot 8\\pi (24) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the ball is decreasing at a rate of $16{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-2876/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-2876"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-315860/field-1/d18a1bb9600f02e78f24b67126dbeff3638a4a289fa1abd25053ab6fa1636c52" :answer/id #uuid "7655f309-0c8f-5637-adf2-c9df7010bd2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-315860/field-1/41f88d47274a71c2d796e6559ccca7d98e82472da0fdd2714f2a384b156a05d8" :answer/id #uuid "6ace003c-0c0a-5479-be83-a5c3e50e7ccf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-315860/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d56df562-06cf-5859-ade6-71d56493f1cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-315860/field-1/d18a1bb9600f02e78f24b67126dbeff3638a4a289fa1abd25053ab6fa1636c52" :answer-field/choices ["q-315860/field-1/d18a1bb9600f02e78f24b67126dbeff3638a4a289fa1abd25053ab6fa1636c52" "q-315860/field-1/41f88d47274a71c2d796e6559ccca7d98e82472da0fdd2714f2a384b156a05d8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-315860" :question/id #uuid "66dac43f-c2e2-5767-925b-2e5479d07e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-315860" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/e850cd14f3b4f791c79c136d0fc799fd17fdc23eeab37e67ad2811f3ff7b6d0e.png)\n\nA snowball is melting in the sun and slowly shrinking. Determine the rate at which the surface area of the snowball is decreasing, given that the radius is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s}$ and its surface area at that moment is $100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the snowball and let $A$ be its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $A=100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the surface area of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then calculate the value of $r$ using $A=100\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 100\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince radius cannot be negative, we have $r=5.$\n\nWe now substitute $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $r=5$ into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\pi \\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of $8\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-315860/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-315860"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344751/field-1/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer/id #uuid "9378ce20-35ee-52c0-8627-1134eea521d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344751/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4e8fd4b-f1a1-5680-ae2a-76aa675acb76" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344751/field-1/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c" :answer-field/choices ["q-344751/field-1/0fbe558572ecce67593db00d22f0d8d9d09c98d019451cd13488790a441e6c9c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344751" :question/id #uuid "8ef823c2-17e3-576d-a12b-1eff5e378c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-344751" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/287add661cff085d1090295f67f87ee80eeaa3d8301ee6632ab54a812a6cd119.png)\n\nA spherical hailstone is melting uniformly, causing its radius to change at a rate of $\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.$ If the radius at a certain moment is $15\\,\\text{mm},$ what is the rate at which the surface area is decreasing?\n\nThe surface area of the hailstone is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the hailstone and $A$ its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=15\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the surface area formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}8\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }$ and $r=15.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\cdot 8\\pi (15) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the hailstone is decreasing at a rate of $20{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344751/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344751"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344744/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "534319e5-85df-5395-8919-f67ed7b2f174" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344744/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "f8f39765-64d6-50ee-bcd5-f63db23c0454" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344744/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f86b5cd-58c4-5d14-8dcb-a977ed2fc7e2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344744/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-344744/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-344744/field-1/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344744" :question/id #uuid "ad7ac8e7-4074-5114-b1eb-c69db9309f47" :question/math-academy-id "q-344744" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/9854b8b086ef6f60f02a37f2a0885cbcdd41ec24d5548abe1aa2d6fbebe8dbfe.png)\n\nA spherical balloon is being inflated, causing its enclosed volume to increase at a rate of $128\\pi \\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the radius increasing at the moment when the radius is $4\\text{ }\\text{cm}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe radius of the balloon is increasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=128\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the volume of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=128\\pi $ and $r=4,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}128\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (4)^{2} \\\\ 128\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 64\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when the radius of the balloon is $4\\,\\text{cm},$ the radius is increasing at a rate of $2$ $\\text{ }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344744/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344744"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-344749/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "d693c65e-271f-565c-a795-0f8f53212cad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-344749/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b38829df-aba3-5a9f-87e7-2a583ca7fa15" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-344749/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-344749/field-1/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-344749" :question/id #uuid "ec2e9a04-0ac2-5fac-9d3b-24868574e1b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-344749" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/e1aafeb4abd5ee1ee545474ed353bccbfb0986cefccb33779fa5744d735d1bdd.png)\n\nA spherical balloon is inflated at the rate of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ If at a particular moment the balloon's radius is changing at the rate $\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\text{ cm/s},$ then what is the radius of the balloon at this moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=48{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $r$ when $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\text{ cm/s}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=48$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{3}{4\\pi },$ and solve for $r.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\cdot 4\\pi (r)^{2} \\\\ 48 & =3{r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\ r & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius at that moment is $4\\text{ }\\text{ cm}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-344749/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-344749"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-226"] :question/worked-solution "Let $r$  be the radius, $A$ the area, and $C$ the circumference of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A = \\pi r^2,$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA =  \\pi r^2 =  \\pi \\left(\\dfrac{C}{2\\pi} \\right)^2 = \\dfrac{C^2}{4\\pi}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\dfrac{\\textrm d C}{\\textrm d t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{C}{2\\pi } \\\\ C & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $C = 2\\pi r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi r & =\\pi  \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-225"] :question/problem "A circular oil spillage is being cleaned up by a team using specialized cleaning equipment. If the area of the circle is *decreasing* at a rate of $6\\pi\\,\\text{m}^2/\\text{h}$, what is the rate at which the radius is decreasing at the instant when the radius is $2\\,\\text{m}?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/d769569ed7854f4d6eb085853b51eb0e5aab0e14cfc3bcdf1e5a480d996ccc9e.png)" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}$ at the moment when $r=2,$ and we are also told that  $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = -6\\pi.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm dA}{\\textrm dt} = -6\\pi$ and $r=2,$ and solve for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi (2) \\\\ -6\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius is decreasing at a rate of $\\dfrac{3}{2}$ meter per hour." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-227"] :question/problem "Air is leaking from a spherical balloon at the rate of $500\\pi\\,\\text{cm}^3/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the radius of the balloon decreasing when the radius is $10\\,\\text{cm}?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065925/assets/460fd4059be2c655e01b89db2738225a50578b5c76bbe048f83312f0bff655ec.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt} = -500\\pi\\,\\text{cm}^3/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm dr}{\\textrm dt}$ when $r=10\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\dfrac{\\textrm dV}{\\textrm dt} = -500\\pi$ and $r=10,$ and solve for $\\dfrac{\\textrm dr}{\\textrm dt}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-500\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (10)^{2} \\\\ -500\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 400\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the balloon is decreasing at a rate of $1.25\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}$ when the radius is $10\\,\\text{cm}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
